-- Azure SQL migration 0008: the data taxonomy (D-37, settled by D-41; document 22, B22-14).
-- Generated by SqlServerSchemaScript.GenerateDataTaxonomy from the logical model compiled into the engine core (model 0.1.0).
-- Do not edit by hand: run the local server's schema-script command, or the store tests with PRODUCT_WRITE_SQL=1.
--
-- A deployed database has run 0001 to 0007 and never runs them again, so this script adds what the data taxonomy needs:
--   1. A table for each data taxonomy entity (C47, C48, C49, C50, O25, O26, O27), in the shape of 0001:
--      system-versioned temporal tables with a history table, like the other class E and V tables.
--   2. The subject check of O06 Owner Assignment (document 22, section 2.6): exactly one subject column
--      holds a value, and Subject Kind names it. An empty Subject Kind means the first subject, so every row written
--      before the data taxonomy, which names its application only, passes. The check reads the row's JSON as the store
--      writes it, and the store makes the same check before a write reaches the database (EntityInfo.SubjectCheck).
--      O09 Decision's subjects stay the engine's to check: its tools also write Subject Application beside a subject of
--      another kind, and its frozen ledger rows could never be corrected.
--   3. The one security policy of 0002, dropped before the subject check changes a table it covers and created again
--      over every table, the new ones included, because a schema-bound policy names each table it covers. The
--      predicate functions of 0002 are unchanged.
--   4. The app's reads of the new history tables denied, as 0003 does for the others. The schema grants of 0003 cover
--      the new tables; none of them is in the list the app may never delete from.
-- Every statement checks before it acts, so the script can run again after a stop part way.

